  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the southeast US will dominate through\nthe weekend and much of next week. This will keep east to\nsoutheasterly surface winds pumping low level moisture into the\narea. For the weekend, the mid-levels look quiet as high pressure\ncurrently in place starts to shift eastward late in the weekend.\nThere is a minor wave of vorticity noted especially around 700 mb\ntomorrow that could be enough to trigger a few showers, mainly\ninland, but we're not expecting much as the layer moisture is\npretty limited. By Monday into Tuesday though, the mid-level \npattern is transitioning and we'll get some weak surface\nshortwaves rotating around the ridge as well. This will give us \nour next notable chance of rain Monday night into Tuesday with \nmedium chances (50-60%). At this time, there's no notable frontal \nboundaries in our near future, and temperatures remain pretty \nsteady around seasonal levels, except Tuesday when we'll likely be\na bit cooler.",
